      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The proximal humeral fracture (PHF) (5\u2009%) of the elderly is the third most common fracture after proximal femoral and distal radius fractures. Proximal femoral fractures often lead to a loss of autonomy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "The aim of this study is to show how PHF changes the patient's autonomy and the coping with everyday life as well as which factors influence the outcome 1 year (y) after surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Data of 62 patients with surgical treatment of a PHF ≥ 60\u2009y was prospectively collected. With a telephone interview Short Form (SF) 12 (physical and mental health; PH, MH), Barthel Index (BI), range of motion, pain, and satisfaction was observed after 3 and 12 months. The dependence of outcome on different factors was investigated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The mean age was 73.3\u2009y (median 73, 60-94). Mortality after 3 months was 3\u2009% and after 1\u2009y 11\u2009%. The PH before the injury (47.9) was significantly better than after 3 months (37.1) and after 1\u2009y (42.6). The MH showed no difference. The BI before the injury (92) was significantly better than after 3 months (86), but the same after 1\u2009y (91). After 1\u2009y >\u200950\u2009% were able to abduct and flex the arm >\u200990°. More than two-thirds were able to perform everyday life activities for body care and nutrition after 1\u2009y. Approximately, 73\u2009% of the patients had little or no pain, and 84\u2009% were satisfied with the result after 1\u2009y. Good score values before the fracture resulted in better outcome. Higher severity in fracture led to a higher level of pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "A surgically treated PHF in the elderly does not lead to a relevant impairment in quality of life. Despite the lack of complete retrieval of range of motion patients achieve a good to very good result in coping with everyday life."
        }
      ]},
